2. Character assets of Mint — additional conditions (NOT Apache 2.0)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The following are the character assets of Mint. They are NOT covered
by the Apache License 2.0. Without prior written permission from
the repository owner, you may NOT modify, reuse (including embedding
in another product), redistribute, or create derivative works from
any of them. Full conditions, including what is allowed and how to
ask for permission, are in docs/character-license.md.

Assets present in this repository (verified at commit dd883e0):

  a. Thinking image and video — the files that show Mint thinking:
       assets/mint-thinking.gif
       assets/mint-thinking.jpg
       assets/mimint-thinking.mp4
  b. Avatar face — the 9 face states of Mint (Neutral, Happy,
     Listening, Thinking, Speaking, Surprised, Confused, Sleepy,
     Celebrating). The face design (eye and mouth shapes, colours,
     and the 9 core face states) lives in the data file
     assets/mint-face.json. The generic code that reads the file
     and renders the face (crates/mimint-ui/src/face.rs,
     face_design.rs) is Apache 2.0; the 9 emotion codes in
     crates/mimint-core/src/emotion.rs (EmotionCode) are generic
     identifiers, not the design. A neutral default face (generic
     parameters in code, FaceDesign::neutral, Apache 2.0) is used
     when the design file is missing, so a fork can remove the
     character asset by deleting one file.
  c. The character name "Mint" as the name of this character.

NOT in this repository (same conditions apply when they are
published; do not mistake them for repo contents): the 20 looks
(assets/looks/ does not exist in this repository) and the design
sources (design/). They are local materials only at this commit.
The character's TTS voice files are NOT character assets under
these conditions: the current voice is a machine TTS voice, not a
personal voice, so it needs no protection here; this will be
revisited only if a personal voice exists.

3. How to ask for permission
----------------------------
Open an issue on https://github.com/ArtyormSatori/mimint with a
title starting with `[character-permission]`, stating clearly:
which assets you want to use, for what (product / purpose), for
how long, whether you will modify them, and whether the use is
commercial. Permission, when granted, is given in writing (in
the issue or by a written reply from the repository owner), and
only within the scope stated in that written permission; anything
outside it remains prohibited. Permission, when granted, is
non-exclusive, non-transferable, and revocable — unless the
written permission states otherwise. There is no implied
permission: the code being under Apache 2.0, the repository being
public, or the owner not replying, is NOT permission.

